"ugcp1-logic": [
  {q:"In Nyaya inference, 'Vyāpti' refers to:",opts:["The subject of inference","Universal concomitance between sign and signified","The conclusion of argument","Direct perception"],ans:1,exp:"Vyāpti = universal invariable concomitance. 'Wherever smoke, there fire.' Foundation of Anumāna (inference). Without Vyāpti, no valid inference."},
  {q:"Which Pramāna involves gaining knowledge through reliable verbal testimony?",opts:["Pratyaksha","Anumāna","Upamāna","Shabda/Āgama"],ans:3,exp:"Shabda Pramāna (also called Āgama) = knowledge from trustworthy verbal testimony/authority. One of the 4 Pramānas in Nyaya."},
  {q:"First step in Nyaya's 5-step syllogism (Pañcāvayava) is:",opts:["Hetu","Udāharana","Pratijña","Upanaya"],ans:2,exp:"Pratijña = the Proposition/Claim. First step: state what is to be proved. 'The hill has fire.'"},
  {q:"Nyaya school recognizes how many Pramānas (valid sources of knowledge)?",opts:["2","3","4","5"],ans:2,exp:"Nyaya = 4 Pramānas: Pratyaksha (perception), Anumāna (inference), Upamāna (comparison), Shabda (testimony)."},
  {q:"Valid syllogism: All M are P. Some S are M. Conclusion?",opts:["All S are P","No S are P","Some S are P","Some S are not P"],ans:2,exp:"AII-1 syllogism: All M are P + Some S are M → Some S are P. Valid inference."},
  {q:"Anumāna in Nyaya philosophy refers to:",opts:["Direct sensory perception","Inference through signs and universal relationship","Verbal testimony","Comparison-based knowledge"],ans:1,exp:"Anumāna = inference. Knowledge derived through reasoning — using a sign (smoke) and the Vyāpti relationship to infer what is not directly perceived (fire)."},
  {q:"'Post hoc ergo propter hoc' logical fallacy means:",opts:["Attacking the person","Concluding causation from correlation/sequence","Using irrelevant evidence","Overgeneralizing"],ans:1,exp:"Post hoc ergo propter hoc = 'after this, therefore because of this.' Fallacy of assuming that because B followed A, A caused B."},
  {q:"In Nyaya, 'Paksha' refers to:",opts:["The major term to be proved","The subject/locus where property is inferred","The universal relationship","The example"],ans:1,exp:"Paksha = the subject about which inference is made. Example: 'the hill' in 'the hill has fire (inferred).'"},
  {q:"The 3rd step in Nyaya syllogism (Udāharana) serves to:",opts:["State the proposition","Provide the reason","Give a universal example establishing Vyāpti","Apply the universal to the case"],ans:2,exp:"Udāharana = the universal example that establishes the Vyāpti relationship. 'Wherever there is smoke, there is fire, like in a kitchen.'"},
  {q:"Which is an example of Ad Hominem fallacy?",opts:["Using irrelevant statistics","Attacking the person making the argument instead of the argument","Drawing universal conclusion from one example","False dilemma"],ans:1,exp:"Ad Hominem = attacking the person (their character, credibility) instead of addressing the actual argument or evidence they present."},
  {q:"Inductive reasoning goes from:",opts:["General to specific","Specific observations to general conclusion","Theory to hypothesis","Hypothesis to data"],ans:1,exp:"Inductive reasoning = specific observations/examples → generalize to form a conclusion or theory. Opposite of deductive reasoning."},
  {q:"Valid or Invalid: 'No S are M. All P are M. Therefore, no S are P.'",opts:["Valid","Invalid","Depends on content","Cannot determine"],ans:0,exp:"EAE-2 syllogism: No S are M (No M are S by conversion) + All P are M → No S are P. This is a valid logical form."},
  {q:"'Upamāna' Pramāna in Nyaya is knowledge gained through:",opts:["Perception","Inference","Comparison with a previously known object","Verbal testimony"],ans:2,exp:"Upamāna = comparison/analogy. Knowing an unfamiliar object by comparing it with a familiar one based on described similarity."},
  {q:"Argument: 'Students who study more score higher. Therefore, all top scorers studied more than average.' This commits what error?",opts:["Hasty generalization","Ad hominem","Affirming the consequent","False dilemma"],ans:2,exp:"Affirming the consequent: 'If A then B. B. Therefore A.' Studying more → higher scores. But higher scores → studied more is not necessarily valid."},
  {q:"Venn Diagram: All dogs are animals. All animals are living things. Correctly shows:",opts:["Dogs ∩ Animals = partial overlap","Animals is subset of dogs","Dogs is subset of animals, which is subset of living things","Dogs and animals are equal sets"],ans:2,exp:"Dogs ⊂ Animals ⊂ Living Things. Three concentric circles: innermost = dogs, middle = animals, outermost = living things."}
],

